@startuml
!include style/ail.puml
title Tutorial 3 · Storage layout: what can the program read after copy_text?
rectangle "<b>Program reserves eight bytes before calling copy_text</b>\nThe bytes may be uninitialized on entry.\n\n<b>First read the returned CopyStatus.</b>\nThen use the matching case below.\n\n<b>Success: copied (0) permits reading the text</b>\n|= Byte index |= 0 |= 1 |= 2 |= 3 |= 4 |= 5 |= 6 |= 7 |\n| Example | R | E | A | D | Y | ! | ! | 0 |\n\nLength = seven UTF-8 code units at indices 0 through 6.\nIndex 7 holds the final zero. Capacity = all eight bytes.\nREADY!! is one possible text; other spellings are permitted.\n\n<b>Failure: unavailable (1) leaves the output unreadable</b>\n|= Byte index |= 0 |= 1 |= 2 |= 3 |= 4 |= 5 |= 6 |= 7 |\n| Output | X | X | X | X | X | X | X | X |\n\nX means the program leaves this output byte unread,\nincluding when searching for a terminator.\nEarlier contents may have changed; X is not a stored value." as storage
legend bottom
  Box = storage owned by the program (caller); each cell = one u8 byte.
  Read byte positions from left to right. The grids are alternative results of one call.
  The program keeps its allocation in both cases and can always read the returned status.
  Non-normative: C3-C5. Synthetic text-copying example; calls and access paths are omitted.
endlegend
@enduml
